[{:db/id 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-32425960-ceab-536a-8061-1f2469974e99"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115070 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/40/406033f059dc4438ea55d43d2bd00b3b79bb99365a2e4846c995f4a447acfd82.svg)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/be/bed2784d17ee63b805917b492052daa7ef079c1682bbc78468eb10f314a92b1f.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/91/91175ab97472067cd14ffff740bb3a98718436f1e94de7a4a6d7fdfb1b35b81f.svg)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115124 :question/problem "Given the system\n![](images/0e/0e89fc57b6af2ddf2a71246dbadc7feb8425d1e1e0ab8f988e7e8ad97d22cff7.svg)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/3e/3e3e8adb0d6e13f7d3b4c3e450b25a789480ac53189ab92385174688e460b2cc.svg)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/41/4122fb4ef56aa99161b15bc25001ca728a6cf7dae36d5dac0aac9b03dc411685.svg)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6deb5ca1-ae5b-5721-895d-6efa8ac5fdfb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115071 :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/6f/6fa4508463b059ce8ef9a5cfa720de9463e41ecdbfb16f4460870bb6fce444bc.svg)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](images/21/215ff061dc7584055bf607f48d0048cb6bde4dcb1ed028fbf4c3a4bd47d89c25.svg)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-32425960-ceab-536a-8061-1f2469974e99" :question/id #uuid "32425960-ceab-536a-8061-1f2469974e99" :question/math-academy-id "q-32220" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+z=2 \\\\  & y+2z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-z=2 \\\\  & y+2z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+z \\\\ y=-2z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the latter can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40f01e87-54e2-5dd2-9850-20313302577b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40f01e87-54e2-5dd2-9850-20313302577b" :answer-field/id #uuid "40f01e87-54e2-5dd2-9850-20313302577b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3937866-2599-550e-8d7d-230111af4c2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b3937866-2599-550e-8d7d-230111af4c2c" "answer-46e61892-168f-50bc-9184-3087f0aa19c4" "answer-86d4df82-dbcb-5384-b53a-8ca94675a39a" "answer-5e341f4d-4527-53f0-a549-17a212c07c94" "answer-59f7a4dc-26a3-51be-9f66-57bb37e63b92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3937866-2599-550e-8d7d-230111af4c2c" :answer/id #uuid "b3937866-2599-550e-8d7d-230111af4c2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ -2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46e61892-168f-50bc-9184-3087f0aa19c4" :answer/id #uuid "46e61892-168f-50bc-9184-3087f0aa19c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86d4df82-dbcb-5384-b53a-8ca94675a39a" :answer/id #uuid "86d4df82-dbcb-5384-b53a-8ca94675a39a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e341f4d-4527-53f0-a549-17a212c07c94" :answer/id #uuid "5e341f4d-4527-53f0-a549-17a212c07c94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59f7a4dc-26a3-51be-9f66-57bb37e63b92" :answer/id #uuid "59f7a4dc-26a3-51be-9f66-57bb37e63b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6deb5ca1-ae5b-5721-895d-6efa8ac5fdfb" :question/id #uuid "6deb5ca1-ae5b-5721-895d-6efa8ac5fdfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-131730" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+2y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (2-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{2}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-26b28742-5ee2-597a-8428-7062724625e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-26b28742-5ee2-597a-8428-7062724625e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "26b28742-5ee2-597a-8428-7062724625e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-32810e7f-ad8d-5b1e-a7d2-839ff11f12a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5b0d5bd9-5c97-5445-afdc-58640864c07d" "answer-0e30254f-fef5-5030-ad74-62323657f390" "answer-0250b550-0b7c-5c05-b8f5-33da7345f93c" "answer-32810e7f-ad8d-5b1e-a7d2-839ff11f12a9" "answer-e23cbcca-7092-5855-aeeb-dd2de6061a27"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b0d5bd9-5c97-5445-afdc-58640864c07d" :answer/id #uuid "5b0d5bd9-5c97-5445-afdc-58640864c07d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e30254f-fef5-5030-ad74-62323657f390" :answer/id #uuid "0e30254f-fef5-5030-ad74-62323657f390" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0250b550-0b7c-5c05-b8f5-33da7345f93c" :answer/id #uuid "0250b550-0b7c-5c05-b8f5-33da7345f93c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32810e7f-ad8d-5b1e-a7d2-839ff11f12a9" :answer/id #uuid "32810e7f-ad8d-5b1e-a7d2-839ff11f12a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e23cbcca-7092-5855-aeeb-dd2de6061a27" :answer/id #uuid "e23cbcca-7092-5855-aeeb-dd2de6061a27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "k=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116493 :tutorial/content "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z=1, \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n![](images/8a/8a8452587098eed9b697c3999c356359b949429f29ce16af2c81cf728be9480e.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-4z, \\\\ y=-3z, \\\\ z=z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115479 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
